  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-798",
              "name": "Sensitive Credentials Stored in Plaintext `.env` File",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Sensitive Credentials Stored in Plaintext `.env` File"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The repository's API application stores real, active service credentials in a plaintext `.env` file located at `apps/api/.env`. This file contains production-grade secrets — including a MongoDB Atlas connection string with embedded username and password, Cloudinary API key and secret, JWT signing secrets, AES-256 encryption keys, and the platform administrator's email address — with no encryption or secret-management protection."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The repository's API application stores real, active service credentials in a plaintext `.env` file located at `apps/api/.env`. This file contains production-grade secrets — including a MongoDB Atlas connection string with embedded username and password, Cloudinary API key and secret, JWT signing secrets, AES-256 encryption keys, and the platform administrator's email address — with no encryption or secret-management protection.\n\nAn attacker who gains read access to the repository workspace (e.g., through a compromised developer machine, an accidental commit, a CI/CD misconfiguration, or repository hosting exposure) obtains all of the following without additional effort: full read/write access to the MongoDB Atlas database containing all restaurant orders, menus, staff accounts, and customer session data; the ability to forge arbitrary JWT access tokens for any user role (including PLATFORM_ADMIN) using the exposed `JWT_ACCESS_SECRET`; the ability to forge customer table-session tokens using the exposed `TABLE_SESSION_SECRET`; the ability to decrypt all stored NFC table tokens using the exposed `TABLE_TOKEN_KEY`; and full control over the Cloudinary image storage account. The platform admin email address is also disclosed, facilitating targeted phishing and credential-stuffing attacks.\n\n**Immediate actions:**\n1. Rotate every credential present in the `.env` file: MongoDB Atlas password, JWT_ACCESS_SECRET, TABLE_SESSION_SECRET, TABLE_TOKEN_KEY, IP_HASH_SALT, and all Cloudinary credentials. Treat all as compromised.\n2. Change the platform administrator password if `Plh52Nb23G0L2d&wefP98Jyh67` (or a derivative) remains active.\n3. Audit MongoDB Atlas access logs for unexpected read/write activity from the leaked credentials.\n\n**Process improvements:**\n4. Adopt a secrets manager (e.g., HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, Doppler) or OS-level keychain. Never store production secrets in `.env` files, even in ignored files.\n5. Add a pre-commit hook using `gitleaks` or `trufflehog` to prevent accidental secret commits.\n6. For development, use short-lived, scope-limited secrets or mock credentials — never real Atlas/Cloudinary credentials in local `.env` files.\n7. Rotate JWT and session secrets automatically on a schedule; configure the server to accept two concurrent secrets during the rotation window.\n8. Add a CI/CD secrets-scanning step that fails the pipeline if any secret pattern is detected in committed files or environment exports.",
                "markdown": "The repository's API application stores real, active service credentials in a plaintext `.env` file located at `apps/api/.env`. This file contains production-grade secrets — including a MongoDB Atlas connection string with embedded username and password, Cloudinary API key and secret, JWT signing secrets, AES-256 encryption keys, and the platform administrator's email address — with no encryption or secret-management protection.\n\nAn attacker who gains read access to the repository workspace (e.g., through a compromised developer machine, an accidental commit, a CI/CD misconfiguration, or repository hosting exposure) obtains all of the following without additional effort: full read/write access to the MongoDB Atlas database containing all restaurant orders, menus, staff accounts, and customer session data; the ability to forge arbitrary JWT access tokens for any user role (including PLATFORM_ADMIN) using the exposed `JWT_ACCESS_SECRET`; the ability to forge customer table-session tokens using the exposed `TABLE_SESSION_SECRET`; the ability to decrypt all stored NFC table tokens using the exposed `TABLE_TOKEN_KEY`; and full control over the Cloudinary image storage account. The platform admin email address is also disclosed, facilitating targeted phishing and credential-stuffing attacks.\n\n**Immediate actions:**\n1. Rotate every credential present in the `.env` file: MongoDB Atlas password, JWT_ACCESS_SECRET, TABLE_SESSION_SECRET, TABLE_TOKEN_KEY, IP_HASH_SALT, and all Cloudinary credentials. Treat all as compromised.\n2. Change the platform administrator password if `Plh52Nb23G0L2d&wefP98Jyh67` (or a derivative) remains active.\n3. Audit MongoDB Atlas access logs for unexpected read/write activity from the leaked credentials.\n\n**Process improvements:**\n4. Adopt a secrets manager (e.g., HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, Doppler) or OS-level keychain. Never store production secrets in `.env` files, even in ignored files.\n5. Add a pre-commit hook using `gitleaks` or `trufflehog` to prevent accidental secret commits.\n6. For development, use short-lived, scope-limited secrets or mock credentials — never real Atlas/Cloudinary credentials in local `.env` files.\n7. Rotate JWT and session secrets automatically on a schedule; configure the server to accept two concurrent secrets during the rotation window.\n8. Add a CI/CD secrets-scanning step that fails the pipeline if any secret pattern is detected in committed files or environment exports."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "10.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-798",
                  "stride:S",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-400",
              "name": "Missing Maximum Length Constraint on Menu Item Search Query Parameter",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Missing Maximum Length Constraint on Menu Item Search Query Parameter"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The staff-facing menu search endpoint accepts a query string parameter `q` with no upper-length bound. The application compiles this unbounded input into a JavaScript `RegExp` object before passing it to MongoDB, enabling authenticated staff members to submit arbitrarily large strings that cause disproportionate CPU and memory consumption on both the API server and the database node."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The staff-facing menu search endpoint accepts a query string parameter `q` with no upper-length bound. The application compiles this unbounded input into a JavaScript `RegExp` object before passing it to MongoDB, enabling authenticated staff members to submit arbitrarily large strings that cause disproportionate CPU and memory consumption on both the API server and the database node.\n\nAn authenticated staff member (any role that can access the menu — WAITER, CASHIER, KITCHEN, MANAGER, OWNER) can submit a very long query string (e.g., hundreds of kilobytes) to the search endpoint. The API server executes `new RegExp(largeString, 'i')` and then issues a MongoDB `$regex` query against four indexed fields. This can cause elevated CPU usage, slow query execution, or service degradation for other users of the same API process. The attack requires valid staff credentials.\n\nAdd a maximum length constraint to the search schema so that the query string is rejected at the validation layer before any regex or database operation is performed. A limit of 200 characters is consistent with the `reason` field in transition schemas and is more than sufficient for real menu searches. Additionally, consider enforcing a maximum regex pattern size at the `searchItems` service level as defence-in-depth.",
                "markdown": "The staff-facing menu search endpoint accepts a query string parameter `q` with no upper-length bound. The application compiles this unbounded input into a JavaScript `RegExp` object before passing it to MongoDB, enabling authenticated staff members to submit arbitrarily large strings that cause disproportionate CPU and memory consumption on both the API server and the database node.\n\nAn authenticated staff member (any role that can access the menu — WAITER, CASHIER, KITCHEN, MANAGER, OWNER) can submit a very long query string (e.g., hundreds of kilobytes) to the search endpoint. The API server executes `new RegExp(largeString, 'i')` and then issues a MongoDB `$regex` query against four indexed fields. This can cause elevated CPU usage, slow query execution, or service degradation for other users of the same API process. The attack requires valid staff credentials.\n\nAdd a maximum length constraint to the search schema so that the query string is rejected at the validation layer before any regex or database operation is performed. A limit of 200 characters is consistent with the `reason` field in transition schemas and is more than sufficient for real menu searches. Additionally, consider enforcing a maximum regex pattern size at the `searchItems` service level as defence-in-depth."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-400",
                  "stride:D"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-863",
              "name": "Missing `REFUNDED` Guard in `confirmCashPayment` Allows Payment Status Manipulation After Refund",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Missing `REFUNDED` Guard in `confirmCashPayment` Allows Payment Status Manipulation After Refund"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `confirmCashPayment` function in `order.service.ts` only guards against re-payment when `paymentStatus === PAID`. It does not guard against `paymentStatus === REFUNDED`, so a cashier who issues a refund can immediately call the cash-confirmation endpoint again to silently flip the payment status back to `PAID` — effectively erasing the refund from the live record without any audit trail."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `confirmCashPayment` function in `order.service.ts` only guards against re-payment when `paymentStatus === PAID`. It does not guard against `paymentStatus === REFUNDED`, so a cashier who issues a refund can immediately call the cash-confirmation endpoint again to silently flip the payment status back to `PAID` — effectively erasing the refund from the live record without any audit trail.\n\nA cashier (or manager/owner) can use this to commit financial record fraud: refund an order to the customer, then re-mark it as paid so the system shows no refund ever occurred. The re-payment occurs in a code path that contains **no `writeAudit` call**, so it is invisible to the audit log. This corrupts financial integrity and enables concealment of cash misappropriation. Every cash order on any restaurant whose `kitchenStartsBeforePayment` flag is set — or any CASH_PENDING order that has already been confirmed — is affected.\n\nAdd an explicit guard for `PaymentStatus.REFUNDED` immediately after the existing `PAID` guard in `confirmCashPayment`. A refunded order represents a completed financial reversal; re-confirming it as paid must be rejected with a clear error, just as a double-payment is rejected today. Additionally, the non-`CASH_PENDING` branch of `confirmCashPayment` should call `writeAudit` with `AuditAction.CASH_CONFIRMED` so that every successful cash confirmation — including in `kitchenStartsBeforePayment = true` mode — is captured in the audit log, matching the behavior of the `transitionOrder` path.",
                "markdown": "The `confirmCashPayment` function in `order.service.ts` only guards against re-payment when `paymentStatus === PAID`. It does not guard against `paymentStatus === REFUNDED`, so a cashier who issues a refund can immediately call the cash-confirmation endpoint again to silently flip the payment status back to `PAID` — effectively erasing the refund from the live record without any audit trail.\n\nA cashier (or manager/owner) can use this to commit financial record fraud: refund an order to the customer, then re-mark it as paid so the system shows no refund ever occurred. The re-payment occurs in a code path that contains **no `writeAudit` call**, so it is invisible to the audit log. This corrupts financial integrity and enables concealment of cash misappropriation. Every cash order on any restaurant whose `kitchenStartsBeforePayment` flag is set — or any CASH_PENDING order that has already been confirmed — is affected.\n\nAdd an explicit guard for `PaymentStatus.REFUNDED` immediately after the existing `PAID` guard in `confirmCashPayment`. A refunded order represents a completed financial reversal; re-confirming it as paid must be rejected with a clear error, just as a double-payment is rejected today. Additionally, the non-`CASH_PENDING` branch of `confirmCashPayment` should call `writeAudit` with `AuditAction.CASH_CONFIRMED` so that every successful cash confirmation — including in `kitchenStartsBeforePayment = true` mode — is captured in the audit log, matching the behavior of the `transitionOrder` path."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-863",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/863.html"
            }
          ],
          "version": "1.5.3"
        }
      },
