          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "10.0",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "6be4fd3999b1fd72ff5b6afa9999fd1ec73b66f6223cb0cdc0647a7bd2c50351",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "critical",
              "cvss": 10.0,
              "timestamp": "2026-08-19 10:18:02 UTC",
              "target": "Restaurant Webapp — `/workspace/Restaurant-Webapp/apps/api/.env`",
              "endpoint": "apps/api/.env",
              "cwe": "CWE-798",
              "impact": "An attacker who gains read access to the repository workspace (e.g., through a compromised developer machine, an accidental commit, a CI/CD misconfiguration, or repository hosting exposure) obtains all of the following without additional effort: full read/write access to the MongoDB Atlas database containing all restaurant orders, menus, staff accounts, and customer session data; the ability to forge arbitrary JWT access tokens for any user role (including PLATFORM_ADMIN) using the exposed `JWT_ACCESS_SECRET`; the ability to forge customer table-session tokens using the exposed `TABLE_SESSION_SECRET`; the ability to decrypt all stored NFC table tokens using the exposed `TABLE_TOKEN_KEY`; and full control over the Cloudinary image storage account. The platform admin email address is also disclosed, facilitating targeted phishing and credential-stuffing attacks.",
              "technical_analysis": "The file `apps/api/.env` contains the following active credentials in plaintext:\n\n```\nMONGODB_URI=mongodb+srv://shamalkhalidnp_db_user:<password>@jazeera-tech.dcnnewe.mongodb.net/restaurant_dev\nJWT_ACCESS_SECRET=<64-byte hex string>\nTABLE_SESSION_SECRET=<54-char string>\nTABLE_TOKEN_KEY=<base64-encoded 32-byte AES key>\nIP_HASH_SALT=<base64 salt>\nCLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=jrtvkyhp\nCLOUDINARY_API_KEY=177313156823726\nCLOUDINARY_API_SECRET=<secret>\nPLATFORM_ADMIN_EMAIL=shamalkhalidnp@gmail.com\n```\n\nWhile `apps/api/.env` is correctly listed in `.gitignore`, that protection only prevents **accidental git commits** — it does not protect against:\n- Developers sharing the file via other channels (Slack, email, cloud storage)\n- CI/CD pipelines that copy the entire workspace\n- Attackers who gain filesystem access to a developer machine or build server\n- Secrets being captured by container image layers or artifact archives\n\nThe critical risk is `JWT_ACCESS_SECRET`: because the API pins tokens to `HS256` (symmetric HMAC), any party holding this secret can forge a valid access token for any `sub` (user ID), any `role` (including `PLATFORM_ADMIN`), and any `rid` (tenant). A forged PLATFORM_ADMIN token bypasses all RBAC, grants cross-tenant read/write access, and enables full data exfiltration.\n\nSimilarly, `TABLE_SESSION_SECRET` allows forging customer session tokens without scanning an NFC tag, granting unauthorized access to any restaurant's ordering surface. `TABLE_TOKEN_KEY` (AES-256-GCM) allows decrypting every stored NFC table token from the database.\n\nAdditionally, the commented-out line `# PLATFORM_ADMIN_PASSWORD=Plh52Nb23G0L2d&wefP98Jyh67` suggests the password has been used and may still be active, disclosing it to anyone reading the file.",
              "remediation_steps": "**Immediate actions:**\n1. Rotate every credential present in the `.env` file: MongoDB Atlas password, JWT_ACCESS_SECRET, TABLE_SESSION_SECRET, TABLE_TOKEN_KEY, IP_HASH_SALT, and all Cloudinary credentials. Treat all as compromised.\n2. Change the platform administrator password if `Plh52Nb23G0L2d&wefP98Jyh67` (or a derivative) remains active.\n3. Audit MongoDB Atlas access logs for unexpected read/write activity from the leaked credentials.\n\n**Process improvements:**\n4. Adopt a secrets manager (e.g., HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, Doppler) or OS-level keychain. Never store production secrets in `.env` files, even in ignored files.\n5. Add a pre-commit hook using `gitleaks` or `trufflehog` to prevent accidental secret commits.\n6. For development, use short-lived, scope-limited secrets or mock credentials — never real Atlas/Cloudinary credentials in local `.env` files.\n7. Rotate JWT and session secrets automatically on a schedule; configure the server to accept two concurrent secrets during the rotation window.\n8. Add a CI/CD secrets-scanning step that fails the pipeline if any secret pattern is detected in committed files or environment exports.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Obtain the file `apps/api/.env` from the repository workspace.\n2. Extract the value of `JWT_ACCESS_SECRET` from the file.\n3. Use a JWT library to craft a token with `alg=HS256`, `iss=rw-api`, `aud=rw-staff`, `role=PLATFORM_ADMIN`, `sub=<any valid user ObjectId>`, `rid=null`, `tv=0`, and a far-future expiry.\n4. Sign the token with the extracted `JWT_ACCESS_SECRET`.\n5. Issue an HTTP request to any platform-admin-protected endpoint (e.g., `GET /api/v1/platform/restaurants`) with `Authorization: Bearer <forged_token>`.\n6. Observe that the server accepts the token and returns all restaurant data.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
