          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "e40d1d8ce473e5d4a8717a237ee3740d34b0aca6a9784c11eb1a6afe805550a7",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0003",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-08-19 10:22:08 UTC",
              "target": "https://github.com/restaurant-webapp / /workspace/Restaurant-Webapp",
              "endpoint": "/orders/:id/confirm-cash",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-863",
              "impact": "A cashier (or manager/owner) can use this to commit financial record fraud: refund an order to the customer, then re-mark it as paid so the system shows no refund ever occurred. The re-payment occurs in a code path that contains **no `writeAudit` call**, so it is invisible to the audit log. This corrupts financial integrity and enables concealment of cash misappropriation. Every cash order on any restaurant whose `kitchenStartsBeforePayment` flag is set — or any CASH_PENDING order that has already been confirmed — is affected.",
              "technical_analysis": "The vulnerability is a missing guard in `confirmCashPayment` (order.service.ts, line 633). The function has two guard checks before performing state changes:\n\n```typescript title=apps/api/src/modules/orders/order.service.ts startLineNumber=633 endLineNumber=637\n  if (order.paymentStatus === PaymentStatus.PAID) {\n    // Idempotent from the cashier's point of view: a double-click reports the\n    // truth rather than double-recording a payment.\n    throw conflict('This order is already paid')\n  }\n```\n\nThe check correctly blocks a double-payment when the order is already `PAID`, but `PaymentStatus.REFUNDED` is a distinct enum value that is **not** covered. After this guard, the function reaches a `findOneAndUpdate` call (line 649-651):\n\n```typescript title=apps/api/src/modules/orders/order.service.ts startLineNumber=649 endLineNumber=651\n    { _id: order._id, paymentStatus: order.paymentStatus },\n    { $set: { paymentStatus: PaymentStatus.PAID } },\n```\n\nThe filter `paymentStatus: order.paymentStatus` will match a `REFUNDED` order (the attacker's read value is `REFUNDED`, which matches the database), and the update sets it to `PAID`. This branch contains no `writeAudit` call, so the second payment write is completely invisible to the audit log.\n\n**Attack chain:**\n\n1. Customer places a CASH order. In the `kitchenStartsBeforePayment = true` configuration it starts as `status=CONFIRMED, paymentStatus=CASH_PENDING`; in the normal flow it starts as `status=CASH_PENDING, paymentStatus=CASH_PENDING`.\n2. Cashier calls `POST /orders/{id}/confirm-cash` → `paymentStatus` transitions to `PAID` (audit: `CASH_CONFIRMED`).\n3. Cashier calls `POST /orders/{id}/refund` → `paymentStatus` transitions to `REFUNDED` (audit: `ORDER_REFUNDED`). Money is returned to the customer.\n4. Cashier calls `POST /orders/{id}/confirm-cash` again:\n   - Guard at line 633: `paymentStatus === REFUNDED ≠ PAID` → no conflict, falls through.\n   - Guard at line 640: `order.status === CONFIRMED ≠ CASH_PENDING` → falls to the `findOneAndUpdate` branch.\n   - `findOneAndUpdate({ paymentStatus: REFUNDED }, { $set: { paymentStatus: PAID } })` succeeds.\n   - **No `writeAudit` call** — the re-payment is invisible to the audit log.\n5. The financial record now shows `paymentStatus=PAID` with no refund event, despite the customer having received their money back.\n\nThe route is protected by `requireRole(Role.CASHIER, Role.MANAGER, Role.OWNER)`, so only authenticated staff can trigger this; however, a dishonest cashier is the intended threat model for a POS system's financial controls.",
              "remediation_steps": "Add an explicit guard for `PaymentStatus.REFUNDED` immediately after the existing `PAID` guard in `confirmCashPayment`. A refunded order represents a completed financial reversal; re-confirming it as paid must be rejected with a clear error, just as a double-payment is rejected today. Additionally, the non-`CASH_PENDING` branch of `confirmCashPayment` should call `writeAudit` with `AuditAction.CASH_CONFIRMED` so that every successful cash confirmation — including in `kitchenStartsBeforePayment = true` mode — is captured in the audit log, matching the behavior of the `transitionOrder` path.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Authenticate as a CASHIER on a restaurant where `kitchenStartsBeforePayment = true`.\n2. Place a CASH order — it begins with `status=CONFIRMED, paymentStatus=CASH_PENDING`.\n3. Send `POST /orders/{id}/confirm-cash` → response shows `paymentStatus: \"PAID\"`.\n4. Send `POST /orders/{id}/refund` → response shows `paymentStatus: \"REFUNDED\"`.\n5. Send `POST /orders/{id}/confirm-cash` a second time → response returns HTTP 200 with `paymentStatus: \"PAID\"`.\n6. Query the audit log — only two entries exist: `CASH_CONFIRMED` (step 3) and `ORDER_REFUNDED` (step 4). The second re-payment (step 5) has **no audit entry**.\n7. The live order record now reads `paymentStatus: PAID`, concealing the refund entirely.\n\nThe same sequence works for the `kitchenStartsBeforePayment = false` flow: confirm cash (CASH_PENDING → CONFIRMED + PAID via `transitionOrder`), then refund, then confirm-cash again — the order is now `CONFIRMED` so the unguarded `findOneAndUpdate` branch executes exactly as above.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
